e (if the env var is set) or making the same HTTP request (if\n        falling back to orchestrator). However, making multiple HTTP requests for the same key is wasteful and could\n        cause unnecessary load. But it is not a security issue and the end result is the same.\n\n        However, note that the orchestrator call might fail in one thread and succeed in another, leading to inconsistent\n        state? Actually, the function sets `_cached_api_key` only if it gets a key. If one thread fails and the other\n        succeeds, then the second thread will set the key and the first thread will then use the key set by the second\n        (because after the first thread's failed attempt, it will try again? Actually, no: the first thread, after\n        failing to get the key from the orchestrator, will raise an exception. But note: the function does not set `_cached_api_key`\n        on failure. So if the first thread fails and the second thread succeeds, then the first thread will have\n        raised an exception and the second thread will have set the key. Then subsequent calls will use the key.\n\n        However, if both threads fail, then both will raise an exception. This is acceptable.\n\n        But note: the function does not cache a failure. So if the orchestrator is down, every call will try to fetch\n        the key again. This is by design (to recover if the orchestrator comes back) but might be inefficient.\n\n        However, the main issue is the statistics update in `LLMCascade` is not thread-safe.\n\n 2. API key handling and caching:\n\n    - The API key is fetched from:\n        1. Cached value (`_cached_api_key`)\n        2. Environment variable `PERSONAL_OPENROUTER_API_KEY`\n        3. Orchestrator REST API at `{API_BASE}/secrets/PERSONAL_OPENROUTER_API_KEY`\n\n    - The caching is done in a global variable `_cached_api_key` in the function `_get_openrouter_key`.\n\n    - As noted above, the initialization of `_cached_api_key` is not thread-safe for the first set, but since it's\n      idempotent (setting to the same value) it is safe in terms of correctness, though it may cause multiple\n      orchestrator calls.\n\n    - However, note that the orchestrator call is made without any authentication. If the orchestrator is exposed\n      and does not require authentication, then anyone can fetch the API key. But that is a deployment issue.\n\n    - The API key is then used in the OpenRouter request in the `Authorization` header as a Bearer token.\n\n    - There is no indication of the API key being logged. We see in `_call_model`:\n          logger.warning('Model %s returned %d: %s', model, resp.status_code, error_body)\n        This logs the error body, which might contain the API key? Actually, the error body is from OpenRouter and\n        should not contain our API key. But note: we are logging the response text, which in case of an error might\n        include the API key if OpenRouter echoes it back? That is unlikely and would be a bug in OpenRouter.\n\n    - However, we are logging the request headers? No, we are not. We are only logging the response body on error.\n\n    - The API key is not logged in the normal flow.\n\n 3. Error propagation and fallback logic:\n\n    - In `_call_model`:\n        - On non-200 status, we log a warning and return an empty string and an error metadata.\n        - On timeout, connection error, or other exception, we return an empty string and error metadata.\n\n    - In the `query` method of `LLMCascade`:\n        - We try a model. If we get an empty response and there's an error, we try one more time with a different\n          model in the same tier (if available).\n        - If the second try also fails, we move to the next tier.\n\n    - However, note: if the first model in a tier fails, we try a second model in the same tier. But what if all\n      models in the tier fail? We then move to the next tier.\n\n    - This is acceptable.\n\n    - But note: the error metadata might not be propagated in a way that the user can distinguish between a model\n      error and a low confidence. The `query` method returns a result that includes an 'error' field only if all\n      tiers fail. Otherwise, if we get a response (even if low confidence) we return it without an error.\n\n    - This is by design: the cascade is meant to try until it gets a response (or until the max tier) and then\n      use confidence to decide.\n\n    - However, note that if a model returns an empty string (which we treat as an error) but the confidence\n      estimation function is called on an empty string? Actually, in the code:\n\n          if not response_text and call_meta.get('error'):\n              ... # retry once\n          if not response_text:\n              ... # then we continue to next tier (without appending an attempt for this model? Actually, we do append an attempt for the first try and then for the retry)\n\n        But note: after the retry, if we still don't have a response, we do:\n          attempts.append({\n              'tier': tier_name,\n              'model': model,\n              'error': call_meta.get('error', 'empty_response'),\n              'latency_ms': call_meta.get('latency_ms', 0),\n          })\n          continue   # escalate to next tier\n\n        So we do record the failed attempts.\n\n    - Then, when we finally get a response (or exhaust all tiers), we return the result.\n\n    - The confidence estimation function `estimate_confidence` returns a score of 0.0 for an empty string.\n\n    - So if we get an empty string from all tiers, we will return an answer of '' and confidence 0.0, and the\n      'error' field will be set to 'All tiers failed to produce a response.'.\n\n    - This is acceptable.\n\n 4. Model selection and bias prevention:\n\n    - The model selection in a tier is done by `random.choice(models)`. This is uniform random, so no bias.\n\n    - However, note that the tiers are ordered: we try the start